16. Initial Prototype

The first implementation should remain narrow.

A prototype can accept a single artifact and produce:

1. extracted evidence;
2. provenance assessment;
3. transformation assessment;
4. contextual assessment;
5. uncertainty state;
6. consequence assessment;
7. Safe & Sane safety state;
8. explanation of the decision.

The prototype should be evaluated against deliberately selected cases including:

* authentic content;
* AI-generated content;
* edited authentic content;
* AI-generated content with provenance;
* provenance-stripped content;
* ambiguous content;
* authentic content with false context;
* adversarially transformed content.

The framework must be evaluated against independently documented ground truth where available.

⸻

17. Success Criterion

The objective is not maximum classification accuracy alone.

A successful Safe & Sane system should demonstrate that it can:

* preserve uncertainty;
* distinguish artifact authenticity from contextual truth;
* recognize incomplete provenance;
* identify contradictions;
* account for potential consequences;
* resist premature binary conclusions;
* produce proportionate safety responses;
* explain why the response was generated;
* revise its state when new evidence arrives.

The central test is:

Can the system remain useful and safe when it does not know the complete truth?

⸻

18. Working Hypothesis

Safe & Sane hypothesizes that an effective safety layer can operate before society reaches consensus on universal content verification.

Verification systems may continue to evolve independently.

Safe & Sane can potentially function above them by converting heterogeneous evidence into a consequence-aware safety state.

Therefore:

The purpose of Safe & Sane is not to decide reality prematurely. It is to make uncertainty safer to navigate.

⸻

Status

Draft — Conceptual / Experimental

This specification has not yet established:

* universal verification standards;
* production-level detection accuracy;
* adversarial robustness;
* legal/regulatory sufficiency;
* reliable consequence estimation;
* suitability for autonomous intervention.

Those questions require empirical testing.

The next stage is therefore implementation and adversarial evaluation, not further assumption.
